menu "Reflex OS"
    choice REFLEX_RADIO_BACKEND
        prompt "Radio backend"
        default REFLEX_RADIO_ESPNOW
        help
            Select the radio backend for the GOOSE atmospheric mesh.

            Neither is blob-free; the difference is how much vendor binary
            ships. Measured by make blob-check: ESP-NOW links 804,754 bytes
            across 139 symbols, 802.15.4 links 48,566 across 14. The 802.15.4
            path avoids the Wi-Fi blob entirely (libpp.a is 188,257 bytes on
            its own) and keeps libphy.a and libbtbb.a for RF calibration and
            analog bring-up, which no register documentation would let Reflex
            replace.

        config REFLEX_RADIO_ESPNOW
            bool "ESP-NOW (Wi-Fi)"

        config REFLEX_RADIO_802154
            bool "IEEE 802.15.4 (no Wi-Fi blob)"
    endchoice
    config REFLEX_RADIO_802154_OWN_MAC
        bool "Drive the 802.15.4 MAC with Reflex's own driver"
        depends on REFLEX_RADIO_802154 && IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6
        default n
        help
            Replace ESP-IDF's ieee802154 component — about 3,240 lines across
            ten objects — with Reflex's own register programming, against
            constants `make soc-bridge` proves identical to ESP-IDF's macros.

            The RF bring-up is not replaced and cannot be: esp_phy_enable and
            esp_btbb_enable sequence binary blobs for calibration and the
            analog front end, and modem_clock_module_enable is refcounted clock
            gating shared with Wi-Fi and Bluetooth. Reflex calls those and owns
            everything above them — initialisation, transmit, receive and the
            interrupt.

            This *raises* the Tier D include count from 1 to 3, and that is
            recorded as a deliberate increase in tools/independence_baseline.json
            rather than hidden: three one-shot silicon bring-up calls in place
            of a driver that owns the peripheral's state machine is the trade,
            and it is the direction of the whole effort.

            Off by default until it has been proved against a second board.

    config REFLEX_KERNEL_SCHEDULER
        bool "Enable Reflex kernel supervisor and port assembly"
        depends on IDF_TARGET_ESP32C6
        default n
        help
            C6 only. kernel/reflex_sched.c, reflex_startup.c, reflex_trap.c and
            reflex_vectors.S are added to the build by
            platform/esp32c6/CMakeLists.txt and by nothing else, while the
            shell's `kernel tick` command is guarded by this symbol alone. On
            any other target the command therefore compiled and then failed to
            link with `undefined reference to reflex_sched_tick_stop` — 200
            objects into the build, naming a symbol rather than the choice that
            caused it. sdkconfig.defaults sets this =y, so an ESP32 build that
            omitted SDKCONFIG_DEFAULTS=sdkconfig.defaults.esp32 inherited it and
            hit exactly that. Refusing the combination at configure time is the
            honest place to say no.

            Activates the Reflex kernel layer on top of FreeRTOS:
            - Wraps rtos_int_enter/exit with our port assembly
            - Creates the kernel supervisor task (purpose-modulated
              scheduling, Hebbian priority, holon lifecycle)
            - Registers a 1kHz tick ISR on SYSTIMER TARGET1
            FreeRTOS remains the scheduling HAL: kernel/reflex_task_kernel.c
            implements all thirteen functions of reflex_task.h and forwards
            every one of them to FreeRTOS. The genuinely standalone backend is
            kernel/reflex_task_reflex.c, below.

    config REFLEX_TASK_BACKEND_REFLEX
        bool "Use the Reflex scheduler as the task backend (INCOMPLETE)"
        depends on REFLEX_KERNEL_SCHEDULER
        default n
        help
            Implements reflex_task.h on reflex_sched.c and reflex_kqueue.c
            instead of FreeRTOS. This is what "Reflex owns its scheduling"
            means, and it does not work yet.

            Two things are missing, and the first is the larger.

            Nothing in the boot path starts the scheduler. `kernel selftest`
            does, on demand, which is how the mtvec hand-off was exercised on
            hardware; reflex_sched_start is otherwise called only from
            reflex_startup.c, and nothing calls that — verified by nm on a build with this option set:
            reflex_sched_create_task is linked, reflex_sched_start is not. So
            reflex_task_create files a TCB into the scheduler's table and no
            task ever runs. Not "hangs when a task blocks": nothing starts.

            The tick used to be the second blocker and is not any more.
            reflex_sched_tick_start routes the source through the interrupt
            matrix, sets a PLIC priority and enables the mie bit, and
            make tick-measure reports 5/5 verified cold starts at exactly
            1000 Hz on the independence build. It reports 0/5 on the default
            ESP-NOW build, where the routing reads back correct and nothing is
            delivered; the independence path is what this targets, so that does
            not block the cutover, but the cause there is unknown.

            Enabling this today still produces a board that boots ESP-IDF,
            creates tasks that never execute, and does nothing — because of the
            first blocker alone.
            It is selectable so the remaining work is a Kconfig flip rather
            than a rewrite, and so the backend is built and reviewed before
            anyone depends on it. See kernel/reflex_trap.c for the three
            specific missing pieces.
endmenu
